1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:03,465
If you play 18xx, you know the moment.

2
00:00:03,465 --> 00:00:08,492
Halfway through the game, somebody asks: is there another number fifty-seven left?

3
00:00:08,492 --> 00:00:11,670
Or, which green tile can this city upgrade to?

4
00:00:11,670 --> 00:00:15,340
18xx Tiles is built to answer that, fast.

5
00:00:16,854 --> 00:00:19,025
It covers a hundred and eighty games.

6
00:00:19,025 --> 00:00:26,392
That's the whole 18xx.games collection, plus older games and variants from 18xx.net.

7
00:00:26,392 --> 00:00:31,791
Every card shows the designer, the publisher, and how many tiles come in the box.

8
00:00:33,302 --> 00:00:42,866
Pick a game, and you get every tile it uses, drawn cleanly in yellow, green, brown, and gray, with how many of each you should have.

9
00:00:42,866 --> 00:00:48,026
You can filter by color, by cities or towns, or just type a tile number.

10
00:00:49,534 --> 00:00:54,205
Open any tile to see the full drawing, and every game that uses it.

11
00:00:54,205 --> 00:00:59,564
Tile fifty-seven, for example, turns up in a hundred and thirty-four games.

12
00:01:01,086 --> 00:01:06,382
The common tiles page shows the workhorses, the tiles shared by game after game.

13
00:01:06,382 --> 00:01:10,158
Handy when you're building a set, or replacing lost pieces.

14
00:01:11,678 --> 00:01:15,032
Lost a tile number, or making your own copy of a game?

15
00:01:15,032 --> 00:01:19,301
Sign in, and you can print number circles for every tile in a game.

16
00:01:19,301 --> 00:01:24,474
Pick the size, and you get a PDF sheet that's ready for paper or round labels.

17
00:01:25,990 --> 00:01:29,131
And if you spot a tile that's missing, you can submit it.

18
00:01:29,131 --> 00:01:34,052
Every suggestion gets reviewed before it goes live, so the reference stays right.

19
00:01:35,574 --> 00:01:37,239
18xx Tiles.

20
00:01:37,239 --> 00:01:40,777
Every track tile, every game, one place.

21
00:01:40,777 --> 00:01:42,572
Bring it to your next game night.

